  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 224 PM EDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\n===== Friday - Late Next Week =====\n\nHeading into Friday, at least partly cloudy skies should be expected \nas the high to our north continues to strengthen. At the same time, \nan expected surface low should form and rapidly deepen near the NE \nUS. So, as this low strengthens, pre-existing northerly flow will be \nreinforced from this deepening low. This will keep highs nice and \nrelatively cool in the low to mid 70s from Friday and through \nSunday, finishing the weekend. Without a large change in the \nsynoptic pattern, pleasant and sunnier skies will be around as we \nprogress through the weekend.\n\nThe pattern shifts by Monday as an Omega blocking pattern emerges \nand a ridge moves in over KY/IN. As a result, southerly surface flow \nreturns and so do highs in the 80s. Especially by Tuesday/Wednesday, \nmostly sunny skies will allow temperatures to soar into the 80s, \nreaching the upper 80s across our south. To close the forecast \nperiod, there is a consistent signal in LREF guidance between all \nthe long-term models of a strong trough digging through the central \nUS by late Wednesday into Thursday. Something to keep in mind is \nthat as the trough approaches, storms may return with the added heat \nby midweek and CWA-wide rain chances. Rainfall totals are too \nuncertain to predict now, although model solutions are right-skewed \ntowards more than an inch possible across the CWA.",
